7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,670
No one knew how he did it.

8
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:22,890
A famous analyst even offered a one-thousand-dollar bounty

9
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:25,110
to anyone who could repeat the trick on

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,680
a real Nintendo console.

11
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,370
For years, thousands of players tried.

12
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:32,550
Not a single person succeeded.

13
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:33,900
Why?

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,870
Because the player didn't press a secret button.

15
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,990
The culprit came from outer space.

16
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,070
A single subatomic particle, born in an exploding

17
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:46,990
star thousands of light-years away, crashed through the

18
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:49,550
roof and flipped a single zero into a

19
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,350
one inside the console's memory.

20
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:55,350
First, we'll look inside the microscopic bucket that

21
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,060
holds a single bit of memory.

22
00:00:57,147 --> 00:00:59,430
Then, we'll see how a particle from deep

23
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,550
space can tip that bucket over, changing an

24
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:05,030
election in Belgium and nearly crashing a passenger

25
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,670
jet in two thousand eight.

26
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:09,920
And finally, we'll see the clever math engineers

27
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:11,326
use to catch the universe in the act.

28
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,640
When speedrunners couldn't repeat the teleport by hand,

29
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,310
a programmer named pannenkoek decided to work backward

30
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,580
from the console's memory.

31
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:25,370
Inside the Nintendo Sixty-Four, Mario's vertical height is

32
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:29,220
stored as a thirty-two-bit floating-point number.

33
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:32,170
Just before the teleport, the first byte of

34
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:35,780
his height was the hexadecimal number C-five.

35
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:42,123
In binary, that's one, one, zero, zero, zero,

36
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,590
one, zero, one.

37
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:48,017
That corresponds to a height of minus-sixteen hundred

38
00:01:48,012 --> 00:01:49,440
and fifty-eight units.

39
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,680
When the programmer manually flipped just the very

40
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:54,480
last bit of that byte from a one

41
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:58,250
to a zero, changing C-five into C-four, the

42
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:02,817
height number instantly jumped to minus-eight hundred and

43
00:02:02,685 --> 00:02:02,870
ninety-five.

44
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:05,780
Mario snapped straight up onto the higher platform,

45
00:02:06,010 --> 00:02:09,650
matching the famous video frame for frame.

46
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:12,790
Nothing in the game's code could ever write

47
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,830
C-four at that moment.

48
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:18,010
Only one thing made sense: something physical had

49
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:21,280
reached inside the memory chip and flipped that

50
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:22,669
single bit by force.

51
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,380
To see how a bit can flip on

52
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:27,830
its own, we have to zoom inside your

53
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:29,400
computer's memory.

54
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,830
We like to think of ones and zeros

55
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:33,270
as abstract math.

56
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,530
In reality, they are physical puddles of electricity.

57
00:02:37,763 --> 00:02:40,940
Dynamic RAM is a gigantic grid of billions

58
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:41,810
of cells.

59
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,920
Each cell is just a microscopic capacitor, which

60
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:47,200
acts like a tiny bucket, paired with a

61
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:50,130
transistor switch that acts as a lid.

62
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,740
When the bucket is filled with electrons, the

63
00:02:52,590 --> 00:02:54,070
computer reads a one.

64
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,740
When the bucket is empty, it reads a

65
00:02:56,795 --> 00:02:57,150
zero.

66
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,860
In the nineteen-seventies, those buckets were comparatively huge,

67
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:02,810
holding millions of electrons.

68
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:06,270
Tipping one over by accident was almost impossible.

69
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,760
But as engineers shrank transistors down to just

70
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:14,900
a few nanometers, the buckets became unimaginably tiny.

71
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,870
In a modern chip, a single memory bucket

72
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,750
holds only a few thousand electrons.

73
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:23,670
The wall separating a zero from a one

74
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:26,560
has become so thin that a tiny splash

75
00:03:26,410 --> 00:03:30,064
of stray charge is enough to overflow the

76
00:03:29,914 --> 00:03:30,064
bucket.

77
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:34,490
So where does that stray charge come from?

78
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,340
Look up at the night sky.

79
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:40,820
Every second, exploding stars and distant black holes

80
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:44,020
blast protons across the galaxy at nearly the

81
00:03:43,870 --> 00:03:45,440
speed of light.

82
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:48,010
These are primary cosmic rays.

83
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:51,340
When one of these high-energy protons slams into

84
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:53,820
a nitrogen or oxygen atom high in Earth's

85
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:57,810
atmosphere, about fifteen kilometers above your head, it

86
00:03:57,955 --> 00:04:00,510
shatters the atom like a cue ball breaking

87
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:02,090
a rack of billiard balls.

88
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,830
That collision triggers a massive cascading shower of

89
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,780
billions of secondary particles, including high-speed neutrons and

90
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:13,850
muons, raining silently down to the ground.

91
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,560
Millions of them pass harmlessly through your roof,

92
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:18,870
your desk, and your own body every single

93
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:19,730
day.

94
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:22,510
If you enjoy stories where deep physics collides

95
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:25,650
with everyday computers, take a second to hit

96
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,040
subscribe, because this is Episode Two of Physics

97
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:30,640
Bugs.

98
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:33,230
When one of those stray neutrons happens to

99
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:35,610
strike the silicon lattice right next to a

100
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:38,680
memory bucket, it rips electrons loose along its

101
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:41,940
track, dumping a sudden trail of electric charge

102
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:44,260
straight into the capacitor.

103
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:47,390
In less than a nanosecond, an empty zero

104
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:49,820
bucket fills up and becomes a one.

105
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:52,478
Engineers call this a Single-Event Upset.

106
00:04:54,278 --> 00:04:56,110
Flipping a bit in a video game is

107
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:57,320
harmless fun.

108
00:04:57,585 --> 00:05:00,330
Flipping a bit in a national election is

109
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:02,230
a very different story.

110
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,800
On May eighteenth, two thousand three, voters in

111
00:05:04,650 --> 00:05:07,740
the town of Schaerbeek, Belgium cast their ballots

112
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:10,260
on electronic voting machines.

113
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,940
When officials checked the results that night, a

114
00:05:12,790 --> 00:05:15,880
candidate named Maria Vindevoghel had received far more

115
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:18,970
votes than was mathematically possible in her district.

116
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:23,080
So election workers pulled the physical magnetic cards

117
00:05:22,930 --> 00:05:25,890
and recounted every ballot by hand.

118
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:30,460
Every single candidate's recount matched the computer, except

119
00:05:30,310 --> 00:05:30,560
hers.

120
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:34,470
The computer had given her exactly four thousand

121
00:05:34,428 --> 00:05:36,060
and ninety-six extra votes.

122
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,630
Why four thousand and ninety-six?

123
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:41,080
To a computer scientist, that number is a

124
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:41,960
smoking gun.

125
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:44,690
In binary, the first bit is worth one,

126
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:48,250
the second is two, then four, eight, sixteen,

127
00:05:48,270 --> 00:05:49,730
all the way up.

128
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:52,920
The thirteenth bit, two to the power of

129
00:05:53,155 --> 00:05:56,790
twelve, is worth exactly four thousand and ninety-six.

130
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,580
The software had no bugs, and the hardware

131
00:05:59,430 --> 00:06:00,920
passed every test.

132
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:03,880
Investigators concluded that a single cosmic ray had

133
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:07,060
struck the machine's memory and flipped bit twelve

134
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:08,558
from a zero to a one.

135
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:12,700
Now ask yourself a scary question.

136
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:16,270
If cosmic rays come from the upper atmosphere,

137
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:19,080
what happens when you fly closer to the

138
00:06:18,930 --> 00:06:20,050
source?

139
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:23,420
At a cruising altitude of thirty-seven thousand feet,

140
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:26,320
the thin air blocks far less radiation.

141
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,640
Up there, the bombardment of cosmic neutrons is

142
00:06:29,490 --> 00:06:32,510
roughly three hundred times stronger than it is

143
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,330
at sea level.

144
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:37,990
On October seventh, two thousand eight, Qantas Flight

145
00:06:38,127 --> 00:06:41,690
Seventy-Two, an Airbus A-three-thirty carrying three hundred and

146
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:45,440
fifteen people from Singapore to Perth, was cruising

147
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:47,740
smoothly in clear skies.

148
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:51,090
Without warning, one of its air data computers,

149
00:06:50,950 --> 00:06:55,950
called ADIRU-one, began spitting out wildly corrupted numbers.

150
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:59,070
A value inside the processor's memory had been

151
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:02,960
mislabeled and misread as a massive forty-two-degree spike

152
00:07:02,810 --> 00:07:05,550
in the plane's angle of attack.

153
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:07,860
Thinking the wings were about to stall, the

154
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:11,300
flight computer violently pitched the nose down, dropping

155
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:13,710
the airliner six hundred and fifty feet in

156
00:07:13,815 --> 00:07:15,060
twenty seconds.

157
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:18,380
The pilots fought back and landed safely, though

158
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:20,680
dozens of passengers were injured.

159
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:23,510
In their official report, investigators found that the

160
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:26,630
hardware glitch had occurred only three times in

161
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:28,760
over one hundred and twenty-eight million hours of

162
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:33,160
flight, pointing to a Single-Event Upset combined with

163
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:35,601
a rare timing gap in the filtering software.

164
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,380
Before we blame outer space for every blue

165
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:41,640
screen on your laptop, we need to do

166
00:07:41,490 --> 00:07:43,610
an honest physics check.

167
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:46,820
First, not every bit flip comes from a

168
00:07:46,670 --> 00:07:48,000
distant supernova.

169
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,070
In the nineteen-seventies, chipmakers at Intel discovered their

170
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,600
own memory chips were flipping bits even deep

171
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:55,790
underground.

172
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:58,430
The culprit turned out to be the ceramic

173
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:00,360
packaging of the chip itself.

174
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:03,090
Trace amounts of uranium in the clay from

175
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:05,330
a river contaminated by an old mine in

176
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:09,510
Colorado were emitting alpha particles right onto the

177
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:10,470
silicon.

178
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:13,080
Second, can we ever prove beyond a shadow

179
00:08:12,930 --> 00:08:15,080
of a doubt that a cosmic ray teleported

180
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:18,550
Mario in twenty thirteen, or flipped that vote

181
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,110
in two thousand three?

182
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:20,190
No.

183
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:24,610
Because a Single-Event Upset leaves zero permanent physical

184
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:26,080
damage on the chip.

185
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,760
Once the memory is overwritten or the power

186
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:31,105
turns off, the crime scene vanishes.

187
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,350
However, IBM measured the threat in a real

188
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:34,880
lab.

189
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:38,020
In the nineteen-nineties, they estimated that a typical

190
00:08:37,870 --> 00:08:40,840
computer suffers roughly one cosmic-ray bit flip per

191
00:08:40,930 --> 00:08:43,470
two hundred and fifty-six megabytes of RAM every

192
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:46,030
month at sea level, and far more on

193
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:47,994
a mountain or an airplane.

194
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:52,165
So if the universe is constantly firing subatomic

195
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:55,620
bullets through our chips, why doesn't the global

196
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:59,880
banking system or the internet collapse every afternoon?

197
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:03,880
Because critical systems use two ingenious defenses.

198
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:08,110
The first is Error-Correcting Code memory, or ECC

199
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:08,570
RAM.

200
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:11,810
Instead of storing just sixty-four buckets of data,

201
00:09:11,870 --> 00:09:14,830
an ECC memory stick adds eight extra buckets

202
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:16,230
on the side.

203
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:19,080
Every time data is written, the chip calculates

204
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:22,630
a mathematical parity fingerprint across those sixty-four bits

205
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:25,600
and stores the fingerprint in the extra eight

206
00:09:25,450 --> 00:09:26,340
buckets.

207
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:29,190
When a cosmic ray flips a single bucket,

208
00:09:29,140 --> 00:09:31,040
the math equation breaks.

209
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,540
In nanoseconds, the memory controller triangulates the exact

210
00:09:34,390 --> 00:09:36,910
row and column that broke, flips the bit

211
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:39,850
back, and keeps running without a crash.

212
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:43,500
And for spacecraft, satellites, and modern flight computers,

213
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:47,640
engineers go even further with Triple Modular Redundancy.

214
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:51,370
Three separate processors calculate the exact same instruction

215
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,410
at the exact same time and hold a

216
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:53,950
vote.

217
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:58,080
If a cosmic ray corrupts Processor A, Processors

218
00:09:57,930 --> 00:09:59,309
B and C outvote it two to one.

219
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:04,160
Most home laptops and gaming consoles skip error-correcting

220
00:10:04,010 --> 00:10:06,080
memory to save a few dollars and a

221
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:07,260
bit of battery life.

222
00:10:07,270 --> 00:10:10,910
Which means the microscopic buckets inside the machine

223
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:14,390
you're watching this on right now are completely

224
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:16,860
unshielded against the cosmos.

225
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:18,880
Most of the time, a flipped bit just

226
00:10:18,730 --> 00:10:20,950
changes the color of a single pixel for

227
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:23,690
a sixtieth of a second, and you never

228
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:24,430
notice.

229
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:27,420
Once in a million times, it crashes your

230
00:10:27,270 --> 00:10:28,030
program.

231
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:31,080
And once in gaming history, it teleports an

232
00:10:30,930 --> 00:10:34,200
Italian plumber through a solid ceiling.

233
00:10:34,265 --> 00:10:37,520
It's a humbling thought: we built machines so

234
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:40,640
small and so sensitive that they feel the

235
00:10:40,490 --> 00:10:44,050
echo of stars that died millions of years

236
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:44,750
ago.

237
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:48,160
This was Episode Two of Physics Bugs.

238
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:51,590
Next time: the day one extra second crashed

239
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,050
half the internet.

240
